# ~/homeserver/config/caddy/Caddyfile
#
# TLS comes from Tailscale, not ACME. Caddy detects the .ts.net name and fetches the
# cert (and renews it) over the mounted tailscaled socket. No port 80, no ACME
# challenge, nothing new exposed to the LAN.
#
# Tailscale issues a cert for the node name ONLY — no wildcards. So services are
# separated by PORT on the one hostname rather than by subdomain. Subpaths were
# rejected because Syncthing's UI calls absolute /rest/... paths and breaks under one.

# Jellyfin — my access, from anywhere on the tailnet, with real HTTPS.
# Roommates keep using http://192.168.1.10:8096 directly on the LAN.
homie.tailnet-name.ts.net {
	reverse_proxy jellyfin:8096
}

# Syncthing admin UI — replaces http://100.64.0.10:8384
homie.tailnet-name.ts.net:8443 {
	reverse_proxy syncthing:8384
}

# qBittorrent WebUI — replaces http://100.64.0.10:8080
# Port 8444, because Tailscale certs cover the node name only (no wildcards), so
# services are separated by port rather than subdomain.
#
# These three header_up lines are REQUIRED — Jellyfin and Syncthing need nothing like
# them. qBittorrent runs two checks that both fail behind a port-based proxy, and each
# one 401s with a bare "Unauthorized" (the real reason is only in its own log, at
# config/qbittorrent/qBittorrent/logs/):
#
#   1. Host validation compares the Host header's PORT against its own listening port.
#      Caddy passes the original Host through, so qBittorrent sees ":8444", compares it
#      to 8080, and rejects: "Invalid Host header, port mismatch". Whitelisting the name
#      in Server Domains does NOT help — the port check is separate.
#   2. CSRF compares Origin/Referer against the target origin derived from Host. Fixing
#      only Host therefore breaks this one instead: "Referer header & Target origin
#      mismatch".
#
# So all three headers are rewritten to the origin qBittorrent believes it is serving:
# http, the whitelisted node name, and its real internal port 8080. Note http:// and
# 8080 are deliberate and not a typo — this is the INTERNAL hop, Caddy to container.
# The browser's connection is still HTTPS on 8444.
#
# THE VPN LANDED 2026-09-02: upstream is now gluetun:8080, because qBittorrent shares
# gluetun's network namespace and the name `qbittorrent` no longer resolves to any IP.
# The three header_up values below did NOT change — qBittorrent's own listening port is
# still 8080; only which container's namespace holds that port changed.
# THE THREE header_up LINES ARE GONE — 2026-09-21. They faked an origin of
# "homie.tailnet-name.ts.net:8080" to satisfy two qBittorrent checks that a port-based
# reverse proxy cannot otherwise pass. Both checks are now switched off at the source
# instead, in qBittorrent.conf:
#
#   WebUI\HostHeaderValidation=false      <- the decisive one. The port check compares
#       the Host header's port against qBittorrent's own listening port (8444 vs 8080)
#       and 401s. It is SEPARATE from reverse-proxy support and is not covered by it —
#       with the rewrites removed but this still on, every request failed
#       "Invalid Host header, port mismatch".
#   WebUI\ReverseProxySupportEnabled=true  <- makes it read X-Forwarded-* rather than
#       inventing an origin from Host, which is what lets the browser's real
#       https://...:8444 Origin pass CSRF untouched.
#
# WHY SWITCH THEM OFF RATHER THAN KEEP FAKING THEM. Both are defences against a
# hostile browser on an untrusted network reaching a WebUI published to the world.
# This one is published on the tailnet address only (see gluetun's ports: block), so
# the network boundary already does that job and the checks were only ever costing
# three lines of rewrite here plus a class of 401 that reports itself as
# "Unauthorized" and hides the real reason in a container log.
#
# THE SIDE EFFECT THAT MATTERS MOST: qBittorrent used to see every request as coming
# from Caddy (172.18.0.6), so its brute-force lockout banned the PROXY — locking out
# every device on the tailnet at once, which is exactly what happened on 2026-09-21.
# With X-Forwarded-For honoured it sees real client IPs, which is also what makes the
# auth-bypass whitelist on 100.64.0.0/10 a real statement rather than a blanket
# exemption for anything that can reach the proxy.
#
# ONE MORE TRAP, in case these ever get re-tightened: WebUI\Address was "*", which
# binds dual-stack and made qBittorrent report peers as "::ffff:172.18.0.6". It
# matches those IPv4-mapped addresses against no IPv4 CIDR at all, so both the
# whitelist and the trusted-proxy list silently never matched. It is 0.0.0.0 now.
homie.tailnet-name.ts.net:8444 {
	reverse_proxy gluetun:8080
}

# n8n editor UI — added with Step 13 (2026-09-19).
# Port 8445, next in the sequence, for the same no-wildcards reason as 8443/8444.
#
# THIS BLOCK IS WHAT MAKES GOOGLE OAUTH POSSIBLE AT ALL. Google refuses http:// redirect
# URIs for anything that is not localhost, so a plain LAN address could never hold the
# Google Calendar credential. The Tailscale-issued cert here gives a real https:// origin,
# which makes the registered redirect URI legal:
#     https://homie.tailnet-name.ts.net:8445/rest/oauth2-credential/callback
#
# No header_up rewrites needed — unlike qBittorrent on 8444, n8n reads X-Forwarded-*
# correctly. It is told there is exactly one proxy hop via N8N_PROXY_HOPS=1.
# Websockets (the editor's live execution view) work through reverse_proxy untouched.
homie.tailnet-name.ts.net:8445 {
	reverse_proxy n8n:5678
}

# The explainer pages, in a real browser — added Step 13b (2026-09-20).
#
# NO HOSTNAME on this block, and no TLS, both deliberate. It is published only on
# 127.0.0.1, so the only way in is an SSH tunnel:
#
#     ssh -L 8446:localhost:8446 homie-lan      # then http://localhost:8446
#
# Plain HTTP is not a downgrade here — SSH is already encrypting the whole hop, and
# a hostname-matched TLS site would reject a browser sending "Host: localhost:8446"
# anyway. There is no ${TAILNET_IP} binding because when this was written the Mac's
# tailnet did not route -- a tailnet-only docs site would have been unreachable from
# the one machine that wanted it. THAT WAS FIXED 2026-09-20 (the cause was an ACL
# granting only autogroup:internet, not either machine), so this binding is now a
# free choice rather than a forced one. Left on localhost deliberately: the docs are
# the one thing with no reason to be on any network. Add a tailnet block if that
# stops being convenient.
#
# The pages render their own SVG diagrams, which is the whole point — bin/homie-docs
# strips those, so this is the only way to actually see the wiring diagram locally.
:8446 {
	root * /srv/docs

	# Serve the Markdown working notes as plain text. Caddy labels .md as
	# text/markdown, which browsers download instead of displaying -- so the
	# links to them from index.html would fire a save dialog rather than open
	# the page. They are read, not installed; text/plain renders them inline.
	@md path *.md
	header @md Content-Type "text/plain; charset=utf-8"

	file_server browse
}
